---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteMap from '../components/SiteMap.jsx';
import sites from '../data/sites.json';
import iamata from '../data/iamata.json';
import cases from '../data/epidemics-cases.json';
import photos from '../data/photos.json';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { excerpt, formatDate, stopNumber } from '../utils/journal.js';

// Trip order (oldest first) for the itinerary.
const stops = (await getCollection('journal')).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.date.valueOf() - b.data.date.valueOf()
);
---
<BaseLayout title="Ancient Greek Medicine" mode="scholarly">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research Notebook</p>
    <h1>Ancient Greek Medicine</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      How did Greek physicians explain disease, and how did it look beside the
      healing people sought from the gods? This site pairs a close reading of two
      Hippocratic texts with a month of fieldwork at the sanctuaries of Asklepios.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a working research notebook that is the result of a single
      non-expert's research. It is not meant to be read as a completed scholarly
      argument. The textual study and field journal can be found below.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/findings">Read the Findings</a>
      <a class="button" href="/field-journal">Open the Field Journal</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="strands">
    <article class="strand">
      <p class="eyebrow">Strand 1 · The texts</p>
      <h2>Hippocratic medicine: weather, place, and disease</h2>
      <p class="strand__question">
        Do the “constitutions” of <em>Epidemics</em> I and III look like the
        environmental theory of <em>Airs, Waters, Places</em> applied to one place
        over time, or like the raw observation that such a theory could have been
        built from?
      </p>
      <ul class="strand__facts">
        <li><strong>Read in full:</strong> the four constitutions of <em>Epidemics</em> I &amp; III</li>
        <li><strong>Compared with:</strong> a short list of claims from <em>Airs, Waters, Places</em></li>
        <li><strong>Translation:</strong> W. H. S. Jones, Loeb (1923)</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="strand__links">
        <a href="/findings">Findings &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/sources">Sources &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="strand strand--field">
      <p class="eyebrow">Strand 2 · The places [WIP]</p>
      <h2>Two geographies of healing</h2>
      <p class="strand__question">
        The Epidaurian iamata record patients who travelled to the god; the
        <em>Epidemics</em> record a physician who travelled to his patients. What
        does mapping the two show?
      </p>
      <ul class="strand__facts">
        <li><strong>Catalogue:</strong> the {iamata.length} tales of the two best-preserved iamata stelai (IG IV²,1 121–122), with where each petitioner came from</li>
        <li><strong>Mapped against:</strong> the towns and streets where the <em>Epidemics</em> are set</li>
        <li><strong>Fieldwork:</strong> 12 July – 2 August 2026; {stops.length} journal entries, from Messene and Epidauros to Kos and Athens</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="strand__links">
        <a href="/geographies">Two Geographies &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/epigraphy#iamata">Iamata &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/field-journal">Field Journal &rarr;</a>
        <a href="/map">Map &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="strands-note">
    The fieldwork gives context and setting. It is not evidence for the textual
    argument: the places the <em>Epidemics</em> describe were not among the sites
    visited.
  </p>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>The itinerary</h2>
    <p class="home-section__intro">
      {stops.length} field-journal entries, in the order the sites were visited.
    </p>
    <ol class="itinerary">
      {stops.map((e, i) => (
        <li class="itinerary__stop">
          <a href={`/field-journal/${e.slug}`}>
            <span class="itinerary__date">{stopNumber(i + 1)} · {formatDate(e.data.date, 'short')}</span>
            <span class="itinerary__title">{e.data.title}</span>
            <span class="itinerary__excerpt">{excerpt(e.body)}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>The sites on the map</h2>
    <p class="home-section__intro">
      The sites visited, the home towns of the iamata petitioners, and the towns
      where the <em>Epidemics</em> are set. Toggle any layer, or click a marker for
      what is recorded there and a link to the journal entry.
    </p>
    <SiteMap client:only="react" sites={sites} iamata={iamata} cases={cases} photos={photos} />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
